[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127801,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid haemangioma (EH) is a benign vascular proliferation with vessels lined by epithelioid endothelial cells, often with an associated inflammatory component.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127802,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9125/0 Epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127803,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F2Y &amp; XH10T4 Other specified benign cutaneous neoplasms &amp; Epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127804,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia; histiocytoid haemangioma; pseudopyogenic granuloma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127805,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127806,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous lesions have a predilection for the head and neck and the trunk {4008683}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127807,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EHs manifest as single or multiple, persistent, skin-coloured to erythematous papules and nodules. Peripheral eosinophilia is seen in 15% of patients {4008683}. Similar lesions may occur in deeper soft tissue, internal organs, and bone.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127808,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions occur most commonly in young adults (with a slight male predilection), but people of all ages and ethnic groups are affected {4008683}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127809,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. Some cases may be reactive&nbsp;&ndash; related to trauma, acquired arteriovenous fistulas, or congenital arteriovenous malformations {1924276}. EHs may be a heterogeneous group of histologically overlapping disorders, some neoplastic and others reactive to shunting.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127810,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>FOS</em> or <em>FOSB</em> gene rearrangements are commonly present in bone and soft tissue EHs, but they are rare in cutaneous lesions and absent in histologically classic examples of angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia, although these are positive for FOSB by immunohistochemistry {26135557; 29527734}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127811,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127812,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 253,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a lobular proliferation of vascular channels lined by bland epithelioid endothelial cells with abundant eosinophilic or amphophilic cytoplasm and vesicular nuclei that protrude into the lumen. A focal intravascular component may be seen, and rare examples are entirely intravenous {32094426}. Inflammation is typically prominent, consisting of lymphocytes, histiocytes, plasma cells, and numerous eosinophils. Fibrosis occurs in late-stage lesions, which are also characterized by less inflammation and less prominent epithelioid endothelial cells. In many cases, mural damage in a medium-sized to large artery is present.</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, the endothelial cells are positive for blood vascular markers, lack lymphatic markers, and are accompanied by an SMA-positive pericyte layer. The epithelioid endothelial cells do not express EMA or keratins and usually display nuclear staining for FOSB {29527734}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>EH must be distinguished from Kimura disease, which occurs mainly in Asian males, presents with larger and deeper lesions and involvement of lymph nodes, and is often associated with systemic symptoms. Histologically, although the composition of the inflammatory cell infiltrate of Kimura disease is similar to that of EH, germinal centres may be seen and the vascular channels are not lined by epithelioid endothelial cells {6718071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
